course: dictionary
lesson: 2710
title: Hard choices and dilemmas
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A dilemma is a hard choice. Every option has a bad side, so you cannot pick one without losing something. «A dilemma is a choice in which every option has a cost.» The word every is your clue: there is no easy way out.
explain: Ethics lessons use dilemmas to practise thinking. Should you tell the teacher that a friend cheated? Both choices bring trouble, so you weigh the reasons on each side before you decide. «In an ethics olympiad you meet dilemmas where truth, loyalty, kindness and safety pull different ways.»
explain: A moral dilemma is a choice where two good rules pull against each other, like being honest and being kind. Stories use dilemmas too, because the reader wonders what the hero will choose. Before you decide, ask: is there really only one option, or could there be more?
fact: A dilemma is a hard choice between two or more options, and each option has a bad side.
fact: A moral dilemma is a choice where two good rules pull against each other, like being honest and being kind.
fact: In stories, a dilemma builds tension, because the reader wonders what the hero will choose.
fact: A false dilemma, or either-or, gives two options when there are more, such as half and half, taking turns, or a rota.
fact: Useful phrases: face a dilemma, be in a dilemma, the horns of a dilemma.
exercise: «A dilemma is a choice in which every option has a cost.» What is a dilemma? | *A hard choice where every option has a bad side | An easy choice with one clear answer | A game played in teams | A kind of medicine
exercise: «A lifeboat has room for only some people.» Why is this a dilemma? | *Someone must be left out, and every choice has a cost | Lifeboats are always blue | Boats always float, so nothing is hard
exercise: What is a moral dilemma? | *A choice where two good rules pull against each other | A friendly argument about sport | A test with one right answer | A rule that nobody is allowed to break
test: Nadia says, «We can only split the cake in half or give it all away.» Is this a true dilemma or a false dilemma? | A true dilemma, because cake is always hard to share | A true dilemma, because only two options exist | A false dilemma, because cake is not food | *A false dilemma, because there are more options, such as taking turns
test: Why do stories use dilemmas? | To make the story shorter | To stop the hero making any choice | To teach spelling only | *To make the reader wonder what the hero will choose, which builds tension
exam: In a moral dilemma, which two things pull against each other? | Your favourite colour and your shoe size | Two bad rules, such as lying and stealing | *Two good rules, such as being honest and being kind | A rule and a sandwich
